The Security Council,

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity and unity of Haiti, and to the principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Recalling all its previous resolutions on Haiti, in particular resolutions 2476 (2019), 2653 (2022), 2699 (2023), 2700 (2023), 2743 (2024), 2751 (2024) and 2752 (2024), and the statements of its President,

Expressing grave concern at the continuing deterioration of the security, humanitarian, human rights and political situation in Haiti, including persistent and escalating gang violence, kidnappings, sexual and gender-based violence, attacks against children, humanitarian workers, medical personnel and journalists, and the spread of armed groups beyond Port-au-Prince,

Deeply alarmed that, according to the United Nations, over 90 per cent of Port-au-Prince is reportedly under the control of violent gangs, and that the people of Haiti continue to face violence, famine conditions, displacement, and restricted access to basic services, while the humanitarian response remains severely underfunded,

Condemning in the strongest terms the massacres, sexual violence, attacks on children and other serious violations of international humanitarian law and international human rights law committed by armed gangs and criminal networks in Haiti, and stressing the need to bring those responsible to justice,

Expressing deep concern that illicit trafficking and diversion of arms and ammunition to armed gangs and criminal networks in Haiti continue to undermine the rule of law, security and stability, and welcoming the measures imposed by resolution 2752 (2024), including the arms embargo, travel ban and asset freeze,

Recognizing the importance of a Haitian-led and Haitian-owned political process aimed at restoring democratic institutions, organizing credible, inclusive, free and fair elections, combating corruption and impunity, and addressing the root causes of the crisis,

Welcoming the role of the Multinational Security Support (MSS) mission authorized by resolution 2699 (2023) and renewed by resolution 2751 (2024), and expressing appreciation to contributing countries, while noting the need for predictable, sustained and coordinated international support,

Recognizing the work of the United Nations Integrated Office in Haiti (BINUH) and the United Nations country team, and stressing the need for enhanced coordination among the United Nations, the Government of Haiti, the MSS mission, regional organizations, international financial institutions and Member States,

Emphasizing the need to ensure the protection of civilians, safe, timely and unhindered humanitarian access, and full compliance with international humanitarian law and international human rights law, and reaffirming the importance of women’s full, equal and meaningful participation in political and peace processes, as well as the protection of children and persons in vulnerable situations, including internally displaced persons,

Determining that the situation in Haiti continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security in the region,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to extend the mandate of the United Nations Integrated Office in Haiti (BINUH) until 15 July 2026, and decides that BINUH shall, in support of the Government of Haiti and in close coordination with the MSS mission and the United Nations country team, continue to carry out the following tasks:
(a) provide good offices and political facilitation to support an inclusive, Haitian-led political process, including preparations for credible, inclusive, free and fair elections and the restoration of democratic institutions;
(b) support national institutions, including the Haitian National Police, justice, prison, border and customs authorities, to strengthen the rule of law, accountability, anti-corruption, human rights compliance and the safe management of weapons and ammunition;
(c) support implementation of the sanctions and arms embargo regimes established by resolution 2653 (2022) and renewed by resolution 2752 (2024), including through information-sharing, coordination and capacity-building;
(d) facilitate the coordination of international assistance, including humanitarian and protection support, and support the coordination of strategic communications with relevant partners;
(e) monitor, report and advocate on human rights, sexual and gender-based violence, the recruitment and use of children, the protection of internally displaced persons and humanitarian access, and ensure that all United Nations personnel comply with the United Nations zero-tolerance policy on sexual exploitation and abuse;

2. Requests BINUH, in close collaboration with the Government of Haiti, the MSS mission and the United Nations country team, to support a comprehensive approach to dismantling armed gangs and addressing the root causes of violence, while ensuring full respect for international law, including human rights and due process;

3. Calls upon the Government of Haiti to accelerate preparations for elections, strengthen governance, combat corruption and impunity, and ensure the full, equal and meaningful participation of women, youth and civil society in political processes;

4. Condemns in the strongest terms all acts of violence in Haiti, including gang attacks, kidnappings, massacres, sexual and gender-based violence and attacks against children, humanitarian workers, medical personnel, journalists and essential infrastructure; demands the immediate cessation of such violence; and calls upon all parties to ensure safe, timely and unhindered humanitarian access and to comply with international humanitarian law and international human rights law;

5. Calls upon Member States, regional organizations and international financial institutions to provide voluntary contributions, personnel, equipment, training and logistical support to the MSS mission, to increase humanitarian funding and to support the strengthening of Haitian national institutions, in coordination with BINUH and the Government of Haiti;

6. Requests the Secretary-General to conduct a strategic assessment of the United Nations presence in Haiti, in consultation with the Government of Haiti and relevant stakeholders, including options for strengthening United Nations support to Haitian institutions and, as appropriate, a possible transition to a future United Nations mission, and to report to the Security Council by 15 March 2026;

7. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council on the implementation of the BINUH mandate every 90 days, including on progress against clear benchmarks related to political stability, security, rule of law, human rights and humanitarian access, with the first report by 15 October 2025 and a final report by 15 June 2026;

8. Affirms that it shall keep the situation in Haiti under continuous review and expresses its intention to review the BINUH mandate by 15 January 2026, or earlier if necessary, on the basis of the reports of the Secretary-General and developments on the ground;

9. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

